# DTF tier manifest: oci-digest-scope-1120
#   artifact-keeper#5793 (a hosted digest resolves only under the image that
#   holds it), #5794 (tags/list answers `name` with the full request name) and
#   #5169 (a Remote repairs a corrupt stored blob from its upstream). All
#   1.12.0. Profile-set: filesystem / single + upstreams.mock-oci-classify (a
#   raw-body OCI upstream on 172.16.<slot>.83; only its `runnable` image and
#   blobs are used here).
#
# THE BUGS
# * #5793: in a hosted OCI repository every per-digest table is
#   repository-wide, so `GET/HEAD /v2/<repo>/<image>/manifests/<digest>` and
#   `/blobs/<digest>` answered 200 under ANY image name of the repository,
#   including one that was never pushed (its tags/list is 404 NAME_UNKNOWN) and
#   one that holds a different image, directly and through a Virtual. The fix
#   (PR #5859) answers 404 MANIFEST_UNKNOWN / BLOB_UNKNOWN unless the image
#   holds the digest (a tag, a by-digest push, an index live under it, an
#   upload or a mount under it, or a manifest of it that references the blob).
#   Controls (both images): the holder serves the index, its platform child
#   and the layer; the old digest of an overwritten tag stays pullable under
#   its own image (#1681); a blob uploaded under a fresh name HEADs under it
#   before its manifest exists; a manifest PUT under a second name that only
#   references existing blobs is accepted and makes them pullable there; a
#   same-repository mount from a never-pushed name is 202. Cross-repository
#   mounts are not exercised: on per-repository filesystem roots 1.11.13
#   answers 202 to every one of them.
# * #5794: `GET /v2/<repo>/<image>/tags/list` answered `name` with the image
#   path inside the repository (`aktest/tags`) instead of the `<name>` the
#   client addressed (`<repo>/aktest/tags`), unpaginated and with `?n=`, and
#   on a Virtual. Control: the tag set itself.
# * #5169: a Remote OCI repository holds an `oci_blobs` copy of a layer it
#   staged for the inline proxy scan (a plain pull-through only fills the
#   proxy cache, which these serves do not verify). When that stored copy no
#   longer hashes to its digest, the pull was refused with 502
#   STORED_OBJECT_INTEGRITY on every request (main before PR #5215), or, on
#   1.11.x, which does not verify OCI blob serves at all, answered 200 with the
#   corrupt bytes. The fix refetches a blob of up to 4 MiB from the upstream,
#   overwrites the stored object and answers 200 with the digest's bytes.
#   The corruption is staged by rewriting one byte of the stored object on the
#   backend's /data volume (a throwaway postgres:16-alpine container with
#   --volumes-from the backend; the backend image has no shell).
#   Control: the intact staged layer pulls byte-identical before the damage.
#   Hosted keeps its 502 on main; that is not asserted here because 1.11.x
#   serves the corrupt hosted blob with 200 (artifact-keeper#4323, covered by
#   storage-integrity-serve-routes).
#
# Red: ak-backend:1.11.13 (every FIX case; #5169 serves the corrupt bytes) and
#      ak-backend:sha-12808b8 (main 2026-11-02, before #5215, #5838 and #5859;
#      every FIX case; #5169 is the 502).
# Green: ak-backend:sha-6e652be (main with #5859, #5838 and #5215).
COVERS="5793 5794 5169"
PROFILES="storage.filesystem upstreams.mock-oci-classify"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
UPSTREAM_ALLOW_PRIVATE_IPS="true"
